  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest analysis this morning depicts a stalled\nfrontal boundary stalled just off the coast of the Carolinas,\nextending over portions of the Deep South and settling along the\nnorthern Gulf coast. Large-scale cyclonic flow aloft continues\nto swirl over the Great Lakes and southeastern Canada, but some\nweaker vort maxes migrating along the frontal boundary are\nproviding enough lift to promote a broad area of rain and\nembedded showers across the southeastern CONUS and into the\nCarolinas. Some light rain is spreading south of US 70 this\nhour and will likely be present for this morning's commute\nespecially in the vicinity of the Crystal Coast.\n\nPool of low-level moisture trapped under the frontal inversion\nwill keep cloudy and occasionally drizzly conditions in place \nfor most of ENC today, which will also keep temps from climbing \nmuch beyond the low to to mid 70s. Later this afternoon, dry air\nadvecting in with a reinforcing boundary and building high \npressure will begin a clearing trend from NW to SE with TDs \ndiving well into the 50s and even upper 40s inland by this time \nTues AM.\n\nHigh pres builds further tomorrow into Wed, resulting in very \npleasant mild and dry days with highs near climo in the 75-80 \nrange (low 70s OBX), but cooler than normal lows well down into \nthe 50s for the mainland, and perhaps a few 40s for the normally\ncooler sheltered locales. The strongest signal for this is on\nWednesday when the high crests overhead and environment is most\nfavored for radiational cooling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mid-level zonal flow returns Thu into the \nweekend, and this will bring a modest warming trend back to \nENC. SW flow returns by Friday as a broad but weak mid-level low\ndevelops and meanders over the southern plains, bringing a \nreturn of Gulf moisture by the end of the week. There are still\nplenty of details to sort out and some timing differences, but \ngeneral ensemble consensus is that frontal boundary still \nstalled to our south will be pulled northward as one or\npotentially multiple lows develop along it and lift northward.\nThis will bring a return for rain chances, and with the moisture\nhaving Gulf origins some heavier rainfall is not implausible.",
